    ($SATQ$9902d2de$SATQ$, $SATQ$c9e6835c-2255-4929-b4da-6fc380ba028c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8717315b-9b76-417c-ac3a-17928f70e53c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Alvarez theory, developed in 1980 by physicist Luis Walter Alvarez and his geologist son Walter Alvarez, maintained that the secondary effects of an asteroid impact caused many dinosaurs and other animals to die <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> it left unexplored the question of whether unrelated volcanic activity might have also contributed to the mass extinctions.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7a59f43b-8e8e-4744-acac-c6ebf3003546","content":"<p>out but</p>"},{"id":"616192fa-f5c0-44ae-89f5-48809b81ad12","content":"<p>out, but</p>"},{"id":"08e23328-e067-4926-bf3e-2a452b0ac58d","content":"<p>out</p>"},{"id":"64e9ba66-cefd-4d2c-88f8-b54a521a7273","content":"<p>out,&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["616192fa-f5c0-44ae-89f5-48809b81ad12"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;but&rdquo; to join the first main clause (&ldquo;the Alvarez&hellip;out&rdquo;) and the second main clause (&ldquo;it left&hellip;extinctions&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-604-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e6f74b0a-7ef0-472c-838f-f2f76af2f24a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9902d2de","external_id":"c9e6835c-2255-4929-b4da-6fc380ba028c","vaultid":"8717315b-9b76-417c-ac3a-17928f70e53c","uId":"f27cbf74-1a17-4806-a107-4ea791fc2f04","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Alvarez theory, developed in 1980 by physicist Luis Walter Alvarez and his geologist son Walter Alvarez, maintained that the secondary effects of an asteroid impact caused many dinosaurs and other animals to die <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> it left unexplored the question of whether unrelated volcanic activity might have also contributed to the mass extinctions.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7a59f43b-8e8e-4744-acac-c6ebf3003546","content":"<p>out but</p>"},{"id":"616192fa-f5c0-44ae-89f5-48809b81ad12","content":"<p>out, but</p>"},{"id":"08e23328-e067-4926-bf3e-2a452b0ac58d","content":"<p>out</p>"},{"id":"64e9ba66-cefd-4d2c-88f8-b54a521a7273","content":"<p>out,&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["616192fa-f5c0-44ae-89f5-48809b81ad12"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;but&rdquo; to join the first main clause (&ldquo;the Alvarez&hellip;out&rdquo;) and the second main clause (&ldquo;it left&hellip;extinctions&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-604-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e6f74b0a-7ef0-472c-838f-f2f76af2f24a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959339,"updateDate":1691007959339}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959339, 1691007959339),
    ($SATQ$9994ae0d$SATQ$, $SATQ$2944e1e6-569e-4649-8926-4a054cd615dc$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Artist Yto Barrada&rsquo;s exhibition <em>Ways to Baffle the Wind</em> incorporates sculptures, textiles, and films. Barrada&rsquo;s pieces, utilizing elements as disparate as plant-dyed fabrics, wire crab traps filled with stones, and cotton balls dangling above a fan, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the ways humans attempt to organize and regulate nature.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2f477f3c-cf27-43d4-91f9-10db8c4a0c83","content":"<p>explore</p>"},{"id":"dfa55c76-3441-4554-b5d6-0cc2b7c89455","content":"<p>has explored</p>"},{"id":"9fe1c3e4-d1f0-46ad-bb77-43a72f56271e","content":"<p>explores</p>"},{"id":"d9162ac1-0053-4f3c-9748-23aab991fc95","content":"<p>exploring</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2f477f3c-cf27-43d4-91f9-10db8c4a0c83"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The plural verb &ldquo;explore&rdquo; agrees in number with the plural subject &ldquo;Barrada&rsquo;s pieces.&rdquo;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the singular verb phrase &ldquo;has explored&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject &ldquo;Barrada&rsquo;s pieces.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the singular verb &ldquo;explores&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject &ldquo;Barrada&rsquo;s pieces.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the nonfinite participle &ldquo;exploring&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9994ae0d","external_id":"2944e1e6-569e-4649-8926-4a054cd615dc","vaultid":null,"uId":"98bbdd45-b5e6-434b-a510-95ee008c0570","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Artist Yto Barrada&rsquo;s exhibition <em>Ways to Baffle the Wind</em> incorporates sculptures, textiles, and films. Barrada&rsquo;s pieces, utilizing elements as disparate as plant-dyed fabrics, wire crab traps filled with stones, and cotton balls dangling above a fan, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the ways humans attempt to organize and regulate nature.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2f477f3c-cf27-43d4-91f9-10db8c4a0c83","content":"<p>explore</p>"},{"id":"dfa55c76-3441-4554-b5d6-0cc2b7c89455","content":"<p>has explored</p>"},{"id":"9fe1c3e4-d1f0-46ad-bb77-43a72f56271e","content":"<p>explores</p>"},{"id":"d9162ac1-0053-4f3c-9748-23aab991fc95","content":"<p>exploring</p>"}],"keys":["2f477f3c-cf27-43d4-91f9-10db8c4a0c83"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The plural verb &ldquo;explore&rdquo; agrees in number with the plural subject &ldquo;Barrada&rsquo;s pieces.&rdquo;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the singular verb phrase &ldquo;has explored&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject &ldquo;Barrada&rsquo;s pieces.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the singular verb &ldquo;explores&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject &ldquo;Barrada&rsquo;s pieces.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the nonfinite participle &ldquo;exploring&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771167,"updateDate":1776362771167}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771167, 1776362771167),
    ($SATQ$9c3630b9$SATQ$, $SATQ$6e22e8b6-807a-46a1-9fec-d9dc4525dd5a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Using natural debris, such as dried <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> such as plastic bags; and more traditional art supplies, such as tree glue, Ghanaian artist Ed Franklin Gavua creates his striking Yiiiiikakaii African masks, which he hopes can help viewers rethink how waste is used in their communities.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"72fa80b0-acf3-4b01-b75a-916117f70d34","content":"<p>leaves, man-made trash:</p>"},{"id":"6f29e829-9bc0-47a2-8842-68453d35c016","content":"<p>leaves; man-made trash,</p>"},{"id":"99dd32ba-a518-4c4b-bd45-e16731ece6d4","content":"<p>leaves, man-made trash,</p>"},{"id":"524460bd-b555-4c02-a507-33554f93cf89","content":"<p>leaves; man-made trash;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6f29e829-9bc0-47a2-8842-68453d35c016"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of elements in a complex series. It&rsquo;s conventional to use a semicolon to separate items in a complex series with internal punctuation, and in this choice, the semicolon after "leaves" is conventionally used to separate the first item ("natural debris, such as dried leaves") and the second item ("man-made trash, such as plastic bags") in the series of materials used by Gavua. Further, the comma after "trash" correctly separates the noun phrase "man-made trash" from the supplementary phrase ("such as plastic bags") that describes it.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a comma after "leaves" doesn&rsquo;t match the semicolon used later to separate the second and third items in the series ("man-made...bags" and "and...glue"). Additionally, it&rsquo;s not conventional to use a colon in this way to separate a supplementary phrase ("such as plastic bags") from the noun phrase it modifies ("man-made trash"). Choice C is incorrect because a comma after "leaves" doesn&rsquo;t match the semicolon used later to separate the second and third items in the series ("man-made...bags" and "and...glue"). Choice D is incorrect because it&rsquo;s not conventional to use a semicolon in this way to separate a supplementary phrase ("such as plastic bags") from the noun phrase it modifies ("man-made trash").</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9c3630b9","external_id":"6e22e8b6-807a-46a1-9fec-d9dc4525dd5a","vaultid":null,"uId":"28e89717-5bb8-410a-abf4-62805aa8a295","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Using natural debris, such as dried <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> such as plastic bags; and more traditional art supplies, such as tree glue, Ghanaian artist Ed Franklin Gavua creates his striking Yiiiiikakaii African masks, which he hopes can help viewers rethink how waste is used in their communities.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"72fa80b0-acf3-4b01-b75a-916117f70d34","content":"<p>leaves, man-made trash:</p>"},{"id":"6f29e829-9bc0-47a2-8842-68453d35c016","content":"<p>leaves; man-made trash,</p>"},{"id":"99dd32ba-a518-4c4b-bd45-e16731ece6d4","content":"<p>leaves, man-made trash,</p>"},{"id":"524460bd-b555-4c02-a507-33554f93cf89","content":"<p>leaves; man-made trash;</p>"}],"keys":["6f29e829-9bc0-47a2-8842-68453d35c016"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of elements in a complex series. It&rsquo;s conventional to use a semicolon to separate items in a complex series with internal punctuation, and in this choice, the semicolon after \"leaves\" is conventionally used to separate the first item (\"natural debris, such as dried leaves\") and the second item (\"man-made trash, such as plastic bags\") in the series of materials used by Gavua. Further, the comma after \"trash\" correctly separates the noun phrase \"man-made trash\" from the supplementary phrase (\"such as plastic bags\") that describes it.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a comma after \"leaves\" doesn&rsquo;t match the semicolon used later to separate the second and third items in the series (\"man-made...bags\" and \"and...glue\"). Additionally, it&rsquo;s not conventional to use a colon in this way to separate a supplementary phrase (\"such as plastic bags\") from the noun phrase it modifies (\"man-made trash\"). Choice C is incorrect because a comma after \"leaves\" doesn&rsquo;t match the semicolon used later to separate the second and third items in the series (\"man-made...bags\" and \"and...glue\"). Choice D is incorrect because it&rsquo;s not conventional to use a semicolon in this way to separate a supplementary phrase (\"such as plastic bags\") from the noun phrase it modifies (\"man-made trash\").</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$9d4a701b$SATQ$, $SATQ$749a7ffa-6183-458d-8c83-89e35fa7ac25$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Along with carbon dioxide concentration and temperature, light intensity affects the chemical reaction rate of <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> as light intensity increases, so does the rate at which the reactants (water and carbon dioxide) are converted into their products (glucose and oxygen).</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"77f1de6b-82fe-45b0-8b0c-8c2d326e35fb","content":"<p>photosynthesis and</p>"},{"id":"491ba38a-b25b-488d-b013-83a7e3ff2243","content":"<p>photosynthesis<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"89baf0e5-ba2e-4349-a716-0125c11b6000","content":"<p>photosynthesis<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"6a2c2b0d-8d42-428b-9d75-7b94dfe4df4d","content":"<p>photosynthesis</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["89baf0e5-ba2e-4349-a716-0125c11b6000"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation in a sentence. In this choice, a colon is correctly used to mark the boundary between one main clause ("Along with...photosynthesis") and another main clause ("as light...oxygen") and to introduce the following explanation of how light intensity affects photosynthesis.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction ("and"). Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a coordinating conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses ("Along with...photosynthesis" and "as light...oxygen"). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses ("Along with...photosynthesis" and "as light...oxygen") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9d4a701b","external_id":"749a7ffa-6183-458d-8c83-89e35fa7ac25","vaultid":null,"uId":"6ca447ba-ce3b-4b56-a241-924a44b4be20","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Along with carbon dioxide concentration and temperature, light intensity affects the chemical reaction rate of <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> as light intensity increases, so does the rate at which the reactants (water and carbon dioxide) are converted into their products (glucose and oxygen).</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"77f1de6b-82fe-45b0-8b0c-8c2d326e35fb","content":"<p>photosynthesis and</p>"},{"id":"491ba38a-b25b-488d-b013-83a7e3ff2243","content":"<p>photosynthesis<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"89baf0e5-ba2e-4349-a716-0125c11b6000","content":"<p>photosynthesis<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"6a2c2b0d-8d42-428b-9d75-7b94dfe4df4d","content":"<p>photosynthesis</p>"}],"keys":["89baf0e5-ba2e-4349-a716-0125c11b6000"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation in a sentence. In this choice, a colon is correctly used to mark the boundary between one main clause (\"Along with...photosynthesis\") and another main clause (\"as light...oxygen\") and to introduce the following explanation of how light intensity affects photosynthesis.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction (\"and\"). Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a coordinating conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses (\"Along with...photosynthesis\" and \"as light...oxygen\"). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses (\"Along with...photosynthesis\" and \"as light...oxygen\") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$9df6da04$SATQ$, $SATQ$dc7daa8c-11e7-42ab-87f7-ef188fb42866$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Entomologists Yash Sondhi and Samuel Fabian have tried to explain why moths fly erratically around light sources at night. Knowing that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5f8eb44f-c232-45bc-91a9-1db88e989df4","content":"<p>the researchers theorize that moths, mistaking nighttime lights for the Sun, continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights.</p>"},{"id":"306fe37c-e505-4a25-b084-c046af4c8628","content":"<p>the researchers&rsquo; theory is that moths mistake nighttime lights for the Sun, continually trying to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights.</p>"},{"id":"045dfd07-f3ee-4b29-aec1-46882f19c0c3","content":"<p>moths mistake nighttime lights for the Sun and continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights, the researchers theorize.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"34afed3c-a17a-45fd-a9ff-fdee991c06c0","content":"<p>moths continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near nighttime lights, the researchers theorize, mistaking such lights for the Sun.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5f8eb44f-c232-45bc-91a9-1db88e989df4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase "the researchers" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase "knowing&hellip;day." In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the researchers&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;know that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase "the researchers&rsquo; theory" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the researchers&rsquo; theory knows that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun "moths" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that moths know that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun "moths" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that moths know that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9df6da04","external_id":"dc7daa8c-11e7-42ab-87f7-ef188fb42866","vaultid":null,"uId":"13e12dc7-a710-47f2-95f2-25a7f305b295","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Entomologists Yash Sondhi and Samuel Fabian have tried to explain why moths fly erratically around light sources at night. Knowing that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5f8eb44f-c232-45bc-91a9-1db88e989df4","content":"<p>the researchers theorize that moths, mistaking nighttime lights for the Sun, continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights.</p>"},{"id":"306fe37c-e505-4a25-b084-c046af4c8628","content":"<p>the researchers&rsquo; theory is that moths mistake nighttime lights for the Sun, continually trying to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights.</p>"},{"id":"045dfd07-f3ee-4b29-aec1-46882f19c0c3","content":"<p>moths mistake nighttime lights for the Sun and continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights, the researchers theorize.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"34afed3c-a17a-45fd-a9ff-fdee991c06c0","content":"<p>moths continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near nighttime lights, the researchers theorize, mistaking such lights for the Sun.</p>"}],"keys":["5f8eb44f-c232-45bc-91a9-1db88e989df4"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase \"the researchers\" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase \"knowing&hellip;day.\" In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the researchers&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;know that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase \"the researchers&rsquo; theory\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the researchers&rsquo; theory knows that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun \"moths\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that moths know that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun \"moths\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that moths know that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323051,"updateDate":1730147323051}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323051, 1730147323051),
    ($SATQ$9eb43963$SATQ$, $SATQ$d2a292d9-9931-40c0-912f-7cf36f65379e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Water in the North Atlantic Ocean is pushed eastward by powerful winds, but the rotation of Earth and interference from nearby landmasses together cause <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> to swirl into a massive, churning whirlpool&mdash;also called the North Atlantic Gyre&mdash;that spins clockwise.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d6fc1fc5-24a6-4643-8343-60be99dd5b92","content":"<p>these</p>"},{"id":"100cd5e8-537d-4456-9cb0-00d5b20a1745","content":"<p>those</p>"},{"id":"fa7f6710-df37-43be-b743-862fde0358cd","content":"<p>them</p>"},{"id":"aaaeaee3-adf1-4124-9f13-849d6640344f","content":"<p>it</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["aaaeaee3-adf1-4124-9f13-849d6640344f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun-antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun "it" agrees in number with the singular antecedent "water" and clearly indicates that the water swirls into a whirlpool.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The plural pronoun "these" neither agrees in number with the singular antecedent "water" nor clearly indicates that the water&mdash;not another plural noun in the sentence, such as "landmasses" or "winds"&mdash;swirls into a whirlpool. Choice B is incorrect. The plural pronoun "those" neither agrees in number with the singular antecedent "water" nor clearly indicates that the water&mdash;not another plural noun in the sentence, such as "landmasses" or "winds"&mdash;swirls into a whirlpool. Choice C is incorrect. The plural pronoun "them" neither agrees in number with the singular antecedent "water" nor clearly indicates that the water&mdash;not another plural noun in the sentence, such as "landmasses" or "winds"&mdash;swirls into a whirlpool.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9eb43963","external_id":"d2a292d9-9931-40c0-912f-7cf36f65379e","vaultid":null,"uId":"332bd7e5-9902-4f30-b32c-6166d02e5122","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Water in the North Atlantic Ocean is pushed eastward by powerful winds, but the rotation of Earth and interference from nearby landmasses together cause <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> to swirl into a massive, churning whirlpool&mdash;also called the North Atlantic Gyre&mdash;that spins clockwise.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d6fc1fc5-24a6-4643-8343-60be99dd5b92","content":"<p>these</p>"},{"id":"100cd5e8-537d-4456-9cb0-00d5b20a1745","content":"<p>those</p>"},{"id":"fa7f6710-df37-43be-b743-862fde0358cd","content":"<p>them</p>"},{"id":"aaaeaee3-adf1-4124-9f13-849d6640344f","content":"<p>it</p>"}],"keys":["aaaeaee3-adf1-4124-9f13-849d6640344f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun-antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun \"it\" agrees in number with the singular antecedent \"water\" and clearly indicates that the water swirls into a whirlpool.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The plural pronoun \"these\" neither agrees in number with the singular antecedent \"water\" nor clearly indicates that the water&mdash;not another plural noun in the sentence, such as \"landmasses\" or \"winds\"&mdash;swirls into a whirlpool. Choice B is incorrect. The plural pronoun \"those\" neither agrees in number with the singular antecedent \"water\" nor clearly indicates that the water&mdash;not another plural noun in the sentence, such as \"landmasses\" or \"winds\"&mdash;swirls into a whirlpool. Choice C is incorrect. The plural pronoun \"them\" neither agrees in number with the singular antecedent \"water\" nor clearly indicates that the water&mdash;not another plural noun in the sentence, such as \"landmasses\" or \"winds\"&mdash;swirls into a whirlpool.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$9f0ac61d$SATQ$, $SATQ$415cb7f4-e324-4f77-b0c2-e3968e90bc06$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Dolores Huerta&rsquo;s advocacy on behalf of farmworkers was rooted in her experience as a schoolteacher in Stockton, California, in the early 1950s. Hoping to help her students and their families outside the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Huerta left teaching to start the Stockton chapter of the Community Service Organization, a group focused on the needs of local farmworkers.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f3e95e7e-a6ab-465a-970e-f4531bd2a942","content":"<p>classroom.</p>"},{"id":"018f0868-4d9b-402f-8be8-31eabfaafb0f","content":"<p>classroom;</p>"},{"id":"4f50681f-270c-4cf1-beba-fac6729a7a05","content":"<p>classroom,</p>"},{"id":"225ccfb2-280e-4d3d-bcee-a9f351876bf8","content":"<p>classroom</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4f50681f-270c-4cf1-beba-fac6729a7a05"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the introductory participial phrase ("Hoping&hellip;classroom") and the main clause ("Huerta&hellip;farmworkers").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a rhetorically unacceptable sentence fragment beginning with "hoping." Choice B is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between the participial phrase ("Hoping&hellip;classroom") and the main clause ("Huerta&hellip;farmworkers"). Choice D is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the participial phrase ("Hoping&hellip;classroom") and the main clause ("Huerta&hellip;farmworkers").</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9f0ac61d","external_id":"415cb7f4-e324-4f77-b0c2-e3968e90bc06","vaultid":null,"uId":"fad49d9d-db12-4b26-beeb-53c148c47bca","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Dolores Huerta&rsquo;s advocacy on behalf of farmworkers was rooted in her experience as a schoolteacher in Stockton, California, in the early 1950s. Hoping to help her students and their families outside the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Huerta left teaching to start the Stockton chapter of the Community Service Organization, a group focused on the needs of local farmworkers.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f3e95e7e-a6ab-465a-970e-f4531bd2a942","content":"<p>classroom.</p>"},{"id":"018f0868-4d9b-402f-8be8-31eabfaafb0f","content":"<p>classroom;</p>"},{"id":"4f50681f-270c-4cf1-beba-fac6729a7a05","content":"<p>classroom,</p>"},{"id":"225ccfb2-280e-4d3d-bcee-a9f351876bf8","content":"<p>classroom</p>"}],"keys":["4f50681f-270c-4cf1-beba-fac6729a7a05"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the introductory participial phrase (\"Hoping&hellip;classroom\") and the main clause (\"Huerta&hellip;farmworkers\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a rhetorically unacceptable sentence fragment beginning with \"hoping.\" Choice B is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between the participial phrase (\"Hoping&hellip;classroom\") and the main clause (\"Huerta&hellip;farmworkers\"). Choice D is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the participial phrase (\"Hoping&hellip;classroom\") and the main clause (\"Huerta&hellip;farmworkers\").</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$9f737b2a$SATQ$, $SATQ$cf594266-c5ac-414f-abb6-d1d804b6019c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8a227a09-a7d8-437d-bc9c-22bfa6ce4166$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In Death Valley National Park&rsquo;s Racetrack Playa, a flat, dry lakebed, are 162 rocks&mdash;some weighing less than a pound but others almost 700 pounds&mdash;that move periodically from place to place, seemingly of their own volition. Racetrack-like trails in the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> mysterious migration.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bca7d935-30bd-496c-bc94-034f0ee81d2f","content":"<p>playas sediment mark the rock&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"598ef929-a44a-450b-bd25-403762759a9f","content":"<p>playa&rsquo;s sediment mark the rocks</p>"},{"id":"c9d3b1f1-3b39-4c96-8668-a91ccd9299e5","content":"<p>playa&rsquo;s sediment mark the rocks&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"36b3f6e1-cd4d-44ef-a46b-beb7a8a41fbf","content":"<p>playas&rsquo; sediment mark the rocks&rsquo;&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c9d3b1f1-3b39-4c96-8668-a91ccd9299e5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural and possessive nouns. The singular possessive noun &ldquo;playa&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural possessive noun &ldquo;rocks&rsquo;&rdquo; correctly indicate that the sediment is that of one playa (the Racetrack Playa) and that there are multiple rocks that have mysteriously migrated across the sediment. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun &ldquo;playa&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural possessive noun &ldquo;rocks&rsquo;,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;playas&rdquo; and the singular possessive noun &ldquo;rock&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural possessive noun &ldquo;rocks&rsquo;,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;rocks.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun &ldquo;playa&rsquo;s,&rdquo; not the plural possessive noun &ldquo;playas&rsquo;.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-684-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$2aa82922-6981-4a42-b8e6-69556e047046$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9f737b2a","external_id":"cf594266-c5ac-414f-abb6-d1d804b6019c","vaultid":"8a227a09-a7d8-437d-bc9c-22bfa6ce4166","uId":"8b8fe00c-6d9f-4190-8af1-9cc31c857791","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In Death Valley National Park&rsquo;s Racetrack Playa, a flat, dry lakebed, are 162 rocks&mdash;some weighing less than a pound but others almost 700 pounds&mdash;that move periodically from place to place, seemingly of their own volition. Racetrack-like trails in the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> mysterious migration.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bca7d935-30bd-496c-bc94-034f0ee81d2f","content":"<p>playas sediment mark the rock&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"598ef929-a44a-450b-bd25-403762759a9f","content":"<p>playa&rsquo;s sediment mark the rocks</p>"},{"id":"c9d3b1f1-3b39-4c96-8668-a91ccd9299e5","content":"<p>playa&rsquo;s sediment mark the rocks&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"36b3f6e1-cd4d-44ef-a46b-beb7a8a41fbf","content":"<p>playas&rsquo; sediment mark the rocks&rsquo;&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["c9d3b1f1-3b39-4c96-8668-a91ccd9299e5"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural and possessive nouns. The singular possessive noun &ldquo;playa&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural possessive noun &ldquo;rocks&rsquo;&rdquo; correctly indicate that the sediment is that of one playa (the Racetrack Playa) and that there are multiple rocks that have mysteriously migrated across the sediment. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun &ldquo;playa&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural possessive noun &ldquo;rocks&rsquo;,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;playas&rdquo; and the singular possessive noun &ldquo;rock&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural possessive noun &ldquo;rocks&rsquo;,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;rocks.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun &ldquo;playa&rsquo;s,&rdquo; not the plural possessive noun &ldquo;playas&rsquo;.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-684-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"2aa82922-6981-4a42-b8e6-69556e047046","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$a03008de$SATQ$, $SATQ$74f18890-70bc-4a2b-8608-64249104bbee$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Proto-Nilotic language, common ancestor of fifty-five African languages with similar linguistic properties, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> like all protolanguages, hypothetical: there&rsquo;s no direct evidence these ancestral languages actually existed.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d7b8a787-95fc-4e91-92bd-006a3d1d323f","content":"<p>is,</p>"},{"id":"879df683-8d7d-4686-a6c6-72d56c66eee6","content":"<p>are,</p>"},{"id":"bf0a4ce3-5d3a-41c1-b347-f043b89cde5b","content":"<p>have been,</p>"},{"id":"b0baeeff-84a5-428c-9120-84d9730dc87c","content":"<p>were,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d7b8a787-95fc-4e91-92bd-006a3d1d323f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "is" agrees in number with the singular subject "the Proto-Nilotic language."</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb "are" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "the Proto-Nilotic language." Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb "have been" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "the Proto-Nilotic language." Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb "were" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "the Proto-Nilotic language."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a03008de","external_id":"74f18890-70bc-4a2b-8608-64249104bbee","vaultid":null,"uId":"9d839ded-4b9c-4cf1-bc25-91cd358f8717","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Proto-Nilotic language, common ancestor of fifty-five African languages with similar linguistic properties, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> like all protolanguages, hypothetical: there&rsquo;s no direct evidence these ancestral languages actually existed.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d7b8a787-95fc-4e91-92bd-006a3d1d323f","content":"<p>is,</p>"},{"id":"879df683-8d7d-4686-a6c6-72d56c66eee6","content":"<p>are,</p>"},{"id":"bf0a4ce3-5d3a-41c1-b347-f043b89cde5b","content":"<p>have been,</p>"},{"id":"b0baeeff-84a5-428c-9120-84d9730dc87c","content":"<p>were,</p>"}],"keys":["d7b8a787-95fc-4e91-92bd-006a3d1d323f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb \"is\" agrees in number with the singular subject \"the Proto-Nilotic language.\"</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb \"are\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"the Proto-Nilotic language.\" Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb \"have been\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"the Proto-Nilotic language.\" Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb \"were\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"the Proto-Nilotic language.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061178,"updateDate":1755117061178}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061178, 1755117061178),
    ($SATQ$a05cc490$SATQ$, $SATQ$4630a3d6-55e9-46b9-b24d-1aaf315df76b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>With the development of new technologies that use natural resources more efficiently, the overall consumption of those resources might be expected to decrease. Economists have observed that improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> efficiency gains, lowering the cost of use, may increase demand to the extent that resource consumption ultimately rises.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3899fb51-b2ce-4d59-bce2-4f87456966d6","content":"<p>conservation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"12a66281-65b2-4d73-9b5f-1ff8ce8a2380","content":"<p>conservation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> though</p>"},{"id":"fae5f6c2-9df9-4ae9-8611-a8bdcdb7e6e5","content":"<p>conservation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span></p>"},{"id":"6b6826ce-5de1-4d89-8638-e4712fef114b","content":"<p>conservation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fae5f6c2-9df9-4ae9-8611-a8bdcdb7e6e5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb "though" from the preceding main clause ("Economists&hellip;conservation") and uses a semicolon to join the next main clause ("efficiency gains&hellip;rises") to the rest of the sentence. Further, placing the semicolon after "though" indicates that the information in the preceding main clause ("improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource conservation") is contrary to what might be assumed from the information in the previous sentence (resource consumption would be expected to decrease with the development of new, more efficient technologies).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Commas can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate a supplementary word or phrase between two main clauses. Choice B is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the two main clauses ("Economists...though" and "efficiency gains....rises") with appropriate punctuation. Moreover, placing the semicolon after "conservation" illogically indicates that the information in the next clause (gains in efficiency may lead to an increase in resource consumption) is contrary to the information in the previous clause ("improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource conservation"). Choice D is incorrect because placing a comma after "conservation" illogically indicates that the information in the next clause (gains in efficiency may lead to an increase in resource consumption) is contrary to the information in the previous clause ("improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource conservation").</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a05cc490","external_id":"4630a3d6-55e9-46b9-b24d-1aaf315df76b","vaultid":null,"uId":"6935a12c-fdd6-4eb6-9284-f958f32558a0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>With the development of new technologies that use natural resources more efficiently, the overall consumption of those resources might be expected to decrease. Economists have observed that improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> efficiency gains, lowering the cost of use, may increase demand to the extent that resource consumption ultimately rises.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3899fb51-b2ce-4d59-bce2-4f87456966d6","content":"<p>conservation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"12a66281-65b2-4d73-9b5f-1ff8ce8a2380","content":"<p>conservation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> though</p>"},{"id":"fae5f6c2-9df9-4ae9-8611-a8bdcdb7e6e5","content":"<p>conservation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span></p>"},{"id":"6b6826ce-5de1-4d89-8638-e4712fef114b","content":"<p>conservation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though</p>"}],"keys":["fae5f6c2-9df9-4ae9-8611-a8bdcdb7e6e5"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb \"though\" from the preceding main clause (\"Economists&hellip;conservation\") and uses a semicolon to join the next main clause (\"efficiency gains&hellip;rises\") to the rest of the sentence. Further, placing the semicolon after \"though\" indicates that the information in the preceding main clause (\"improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource conservation\") is contrary to what might be assumed from the information in the previous sentence (resource consumption would be expected to decrease with the development of new, more efficient technologies).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Commas can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate a supplementary word or phrase between two main clauses. Choice B is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the two main clauses (\"Economists...though\" and \"efficiency gains....rises\") with appropriate punctuation. Moreover, placing the semicolon after \"conservation\" illogically indicates that the information in the next clause (gains in efficiency may lead to an increase in resource consumption) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (\"improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource conservation\"). Choice D is incorrect because placing a comma after \"conservation\" illogically indicates that the information in the next clause (gains in efficiency may lead to an increase in resource consumption) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (\"improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource conservation\").</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669206,"updateDate":1743430669206}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669206, 1743430669206),
    ($SATQ$a12e3b8a$SATQ$, $SATQ$078ae823-534c-4607-b015-b820dc16b66d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Customers who are satisfied with how a company resolves a service issue may regard that company more positively than they would if no such issue had occurred. This idea is known as the service recovery <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> research suggests that it has important implications for customer loyalty and retention.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"46f73565-35e1-492a-aeec-3e0a23a41606","content":"<p>paradox,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3b0069af-0f21-48fc-b394-f5324ca3c4e3","content":"<p>paradox</p>"},{"id":"ac2d47ba-ddc0-4f5e-8977-ac28bc6909ad","content":"<p>paradox, and</p>"},{"id":"5342eb21-f7cc-4d6b-b991-d221297d0a36","content":"<p>paradox and,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ac2d47ba-ddc0-4f5e-8977-ac28bc6909ad"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction "and" to join the first main clause ("This idea&hellip;paradox") and the second main clause ("research&hellip;retention").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice D is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction, not after it.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a12e3b8a","external_id":"078ae823-534c-4607-b015-b820dc16b66d","vaultid":null,"uId":"e9661596-9fed-4871-8a3e-5497cba2f2b9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Customers who are satisfied with how a company resolves a service issue may regard that company more positively than they would if no such issue had occurred. This idea is known as the service recovery <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> research suggests that it has important implications for customer loyalty and retention.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"46f73565-35e1-492a-aeec-3e0a23a41606","content":"<p>paradox,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3b0069af-0f21-48fc-b394-f5324ca3c4e3","content":"<p>paradox</p>"},{"id":"ac2d47ba-ddc0-4f5e-8977-ac28bc6909ad","content":"<p>paradox, and</p>"},{"id":"5342eb21-f7cc-4d6b-b991-d221297d0a36","content":"<p>paradox and,</p>"}],"keys":["ac2d47ba-ddc0-4f5e-8977-ac28bc6909ad"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction \"and\" to join the first main clause (\"This idea&hellip;paradox\") and the second main clause (\"research&hellip;retention\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice D is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction, not after it.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323050,"updateDate":1730147323050}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323050, 1730147323050),
    ($SATQ$a14eef71$SATQ$, $SATQ$0522e184-5222-478f-9ddb-418cc01f08fe$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$77ce137d-55fb-4e4c-bfd7-7ecc62d5e36e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2015, a team led by materials scientists Anirudha Sumant and Diana Berman succeeded in reducing the coefficient of friction (COF) between two surfaces to the lowest possible level&mdash;superlubricity. A nearly frictionless (and, as its name suggests, extremely slippery) state, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e71d246e-2d2f-4e04-b321-e24e35db5e40","content":"<p>when their COF drops below 0.01, two surfaces reach superlubricity.</p>"},{"id":"a6dc8795-e811-4ba5-8859-a6bcd988a7d8","content":"<p>two surfaces, when their COF drops below 0.01, reach superlubricity.</p>"},{"id":"c25ab915-8cd3-4cc6-8314-8736fe53d0a8","content":"<p>reaching superlubricity occurs when two surfaces&rsquo; COF drops below 0.01.</p>"},{"id":"28b0e584-50b2-4bbf-b431-9ed781ca042f","content":"<p>superlubricity is reached when two surfaces&rsquo; COF drops below 0.01.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["28b0e584-50b2-4bbf-b431-9ed781ca042f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The subject of the modifier &ldquo;a nearly frictionless state&rdquo; is &ldquo;superlubricity.&rdquo; Subject-modifier placement requires a modifier and its subject to be next to one another, so &ldquo;superlubricity&rdquo; must be the first word in the missing clause. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-modifier placement error. The subject of the modifier &ldquo;a nearly frictionless state&rdquo; is &ldquo;superlubricity.&rdquo; Subject-modifier placement requires a modifier and its subject to be next to one another, so &ldquo;superlubricity&rdquo; must be the first word in the missing clause. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-modifier placement error. The subject of the modifier &ldquo;a nearly frictionless state&rdquo; is &ldquo;superlubricity.&rdquo; Subject-modifier placement requires a modifier and its subject to be next to one another, so &ldquo;superlubricity&rdquo; must be the first word in the missing clause. Choice C is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-modifier placement error. The subject of the modifier &ldquo;a nearly frictionless state&rdquo; is &ldquo;superlubricity.&rdquo; Subject-modifier placement requires a modifier and its subject to be next to one another, so &ldquo;superlubricity&rdquo; must be the first word in the missing clause. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-H001$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8092c78c-d6a0-4f72-9e5e-719585511310$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a14eef71","external_id":"0522e184-5222-478f-9ddb-418cc01f08fe","vaultid":"77ce137d-55fb-4e4c-bfd7-7ecc62d5e36e","uId":"d1eca422-44b5-4abd-8441-067f1c07ba8e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2015, a team led by materials scientists Anirudha Sumant and Diana Berman succeeded in reducing the coefficient of friction (COF) between two surfaces to the lowest possible level&mdash;superlubricity. A nearly frictionless (and, as its name suggests, extremely slippery) state, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e71d246e-2d2f-4e04-b321-e24e35db5e40","content":"<p>when their COF drops below 0.01, two surfaces reach superlubricity.</p>"},{"id":"a6dc8795-e811-4ba5-8859-a6bcd988a7d8","content":"<p>two surfaces, when their COF drops below 0.01, reach superlubricity.</p>"},{"id":"c25ab915-8cd3-4cc6-8314-8736fe53d0a8","content":"<p>reaching superlubricity occurs when two surfaces&rsquo; COF drops below 0.01.</p>"},{"id":"28b0e584-50b2-4bbf-b431-9ed781ca042f","content":"<p>superlubricity is reached when two surfaces&rsquo; COF drops below 0.01.</p>"}],"keys":["28b0e584-50b2-4bbf-b431-9ed781ca042f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The subject of the modifier &ldquo;a nearly frictionless state&rdquo; is &ldquo;superlubricity.&rdquo; Subject-modifier placement requires a modifier and its subject to be next to one another, so &ldquo;superlubricity&rdquo; must be the first word in the missing clause. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-modifier placement error. The subject of the modifier &ldquo;a nearly frictionless state&rdquo; is &ldquo;superlubricity.&rdquo; Subject-modifier placement requires a modifier and its subject to be next to one another, so &ldquo;superlubricity&rdquo; must be the first word in the missing clause. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-modifier placement error. The subject of the modifier &ldquo;a nearly frictionless state&rdquo; is &ldquo;superlubricity.&rdquo; Subject-modifier placement requires a modifier and its subject to be next to one another, so &ldquo;superlubricity&rdquo; must be the first word in the missing clause. Choice C is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-modifier placement error. The subject of the modifier &ldquo;a nearly frictionless state&rdquo; is &ldquo;superlubricity.&rdquo; Subject-modifier placement requires a modifier and its subject to be next to one another, so &ldquo;superlubricity&rdquo; must be the first word in the missing clause. </p>","parenttemp