    ($SATQ$a153ad6a$SATQ$, $SATQ$97d6d296-9094-426b-ac81-e4485adb17b9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While light is known as one of the fastest-moving substances, it slows down when passing through some types of matter. One such type of matter is a form of cooled, condensed gas called a Bose-Einstein condensate <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Dutch physicist Lene Hau famously used a BEC to slow a beam of light to a complete halt.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0cc69636-39c9-4686-8bfb-89e7deb992b0","content":"<p>(BEC),</p>"},{"id":"2ecf00fc-822d-4302-a452-08b2d820c220","content":"<p>(BEC) and</p>"},{"id":"e6704d79-2585-4d99-809e-cff4c643633b","content":"<p>(BEC);</p>"},{"id":"07ec3285-76ae-4a39-b218-baeedfafc6d4","content":"<p>(BEC)</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e6704d79-2585-4d99-809e-cff4c643633b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a semicolon to join the first main clause ("One&hellip;(BEC)") and the second main clause ("Dutch&hellip;halt").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. Without a comma preceding it, a conjunction can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a153ad6a","external_id":"97d6d296-9094-426b-ac81-e4485adb17b9","vaultid":null,"uId":"31909e06-41bc-4ea7-bd22-53b94e7412a5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While light is known as one of the fastest-moving substances, it slows down when passing through some types of matter. One such type of matter is a form of cooled, condensed gas called a Bose-Einstein condensate <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Dutch physicist Lene Hau famously used a BEC to slow a beam of light to a complete halt.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0cc69636-39c9-4686-8bfb-89e7deb992b0","content":"<p>(BEC),</p>"},{"id":"2ecf00fc-822d-4302-a452-08b2d820c220","content":"<p>(BEC) and</p>"},{"id":"e6704d79-2585-4d99-809e-cff4c643633b","content":"<p>(BEC);</p>"},{"id":"07ec3285-76ae-4a39-b218-baeedfafc6d4","content":"<p>(BEC)</p>"}],"keys":["e6704d79-2585-4d99-809e-cff4c643633b"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a semicolon to join the first main clause (\"One&hellip;(BEC)\") and the second main clause (\"Dutch&hellip;halt\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. Without a comma preceding it, a conjunction can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$a1e0c981$SATQ$, $SATQ$680f1070-ecf0-4f32-97f8-edc4a018f80c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$dc8ddfae-f734-4558-a57c-b614a51b9c7e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In her book <em>The Woman Warrior: Memoirs of a Girlhood Among Ghosts</em>, author Maxine Hong Kingston examines themes <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> childhood, womanhood, and Chinese American identity by intertwining autobiography and mythology.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"73132b60-7a25-4f23-9f19-c4893cefc05c","content":"<p>of:</p>"},{"id":"1bba0ecd-1f32-4d62-a4bb-c48a681847e9","content":"<p>&nbsp;of</p>"},{"id":"323c2f5f-ea39-4c89-bcf1-d155a3555aa6","content":"<p>of&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"e613556a-2070-4963-84d8-74125b20c00d","content":"<p>of,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1bba0ecd-1f32-4d62-a4bb-c48a681847e9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Themes of childhood&rdquo; is one noun phrase, with &ldquo;themes of&rdquo; implicitly carrying over to the other items on the list (&ldquo;themes of childhood, [themes of] womanhood, and [themes of] Chinese American identity&rdquo;). &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice inappropriately breaks up the introduction of a list. Also, &ldquo;In her book&hellip;themes of&rdquo; is not an independent clause, thanks to the dangling &ldquo;of&rdquo; at the end, so it can&rsquo;t precede a colon.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This choice inappropriately breaks up the introduction of a list. Also, &ldquo;In her book&hellip;themes of&rdquo; is not an independent clause, thanks to the dangling &ldquo;of&rdquo; at the end, so it can&rsquo;t precede a single dash.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. This choice inappropriately breaks up the introduction of a list. &ldquo;Themes of&rdquo; implicitly carries over to each item on the list (&ldquo;themes of childhood, [themes of] womanhood, and [themes of] Chinese American identity&rdquo;), so we don&rsquo;t want to use a comma to separate it.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-E004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$a19c9473-629a-486f-a6ba-6ba4c50c6fd7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a1e0c981","external_id":"680f1070-ecf0-4f32-97f8-edc4a018f80c","vaultid":"dc8ddfae-f734-4558-a57c-b614a51b9c7e","uId":"409cfe03-0bd6-4c19-bdcb-42f4074dd348","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In her book <em>The Woman Warrior: Memoirs of a Girlhood Among Ghosts</em>, author Maxine Hong Kingston examines themes <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> childhood, womanhood, and Chinese American identity by intertwining autobiography and mythology.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"73132b60-7a25-4f23-9f19-c4893cefc05c","content":"<p>of:</p>"},{"id":"1bba0ecd-1f32-4d62-a4bb-c48a681847e9","content":"<p>&nbsp;of</p>"},{"id":"323c2f5f-ea39-4c89-bcf1-d155a3555aa6","content":"<p>of&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"e613556a-2070-4963-84d8-74125b20c00d","content":"<p>of,</p>"}],"keys":["1bba0ecd-1f32-4d62-a4bb-c48a681847e9"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Themes of childhood&rdquo; is one noun phrase, with &ldquo;themes of&rdquo; implicitly carrying over to the other items on the list (&ldquo;themes of childhood, [themes of] womanhood, and [themes of] Chinese American identity&rdquo;). &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice inappropriately breaks up the introduction of a list. Also, &ldquo;In her book&hellip;themes of&rdquo; is not an independent clause, thanks to the dangling &ldquo;of&rdquo; at the end, so it can&rsquo;t precede a colon.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This choice inappropriately breaks up the introduction of a list. Also, &ldquo;In her book&hellip;themes of&rdquo; is not an independent clause, thanks to the dangling &ldquo;of&rdquo; at the end, so it can&rsquo;t precede a single dash.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. This choice inappropriately breaks up the introduction of a list. &ldquo;Themes of&rdquo; implicitly carries over to each item on the list (&ldquo;themes of childhood, [themes of] womanhood, and [themes of] Chinese American identity&rdquo;), so we don&rsquo;t want to use a comma to separate it.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-E004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"a19c9473-629a-486f-a6ba-6ba4c50c6fd7","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959358,"updateDate":1691007959358}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959358, 1691007959358),
    ($SATQ$a2293b3f$SATQ$, $SATQ$5aa98f5c-cc3b-452b-85ea-4fedfc2a58d5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A harpsichord may look just like a piano, but the difference between the two instruments is easy to hear. When a harpsichord&rsquo;s keys are pressed, the strings inside the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> are plucked, not struck.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ef5fae4a-a922-494c-82dd-c0ce30856d6f","content":"<p>instrument:</p>"},{"id":"78054cba-fdb8-4f26-9a21-cdb552ae14fa","content":"<p>instrument</p>"},{"id":"611f48c3-fbb0-46e5-bc4e-ad6be1ca823c","content":"<p>instrument&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"0ab986cc-ee52-4cbe-a332-7497c7f143bb","content":"<p>instrument,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["78054cba-fdb8-4f26-9a21-cdb552ae14fa"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a subject and a verb. When, as in this case, a subject ("the strings inside the instrument") is immediately followed by a main verb ("are plucked"), no punctuation is needed.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a2293b3f","external_id":"5aa98f5c-cc3b-452b-85ea-4fedfc2a58d5","vaultid":null,"uId":"ca5e443e-90a8-413d-bf16-8afc3c6c8b54","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A harpsichord may look just like a piano, but the difference between the two instruments is easy to hear. When a harpsichord&rsquo;s keys are pressed, the strings inside the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> are plucked, not struck.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ef5fae4a-a922-494c-82dd-c0ce30856d6f","content":"<p>instrument:</p>"},{"id":"78054cba-fdb8-4f26-9a21-cdb552ae14fa","content":"<p>instrument</p>"},{"id":"611f48c3-fbb0-46e5-bc4e-ad6be1ca823c","content":"<p>instrument&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"0ab986cc-ee52-4cbe-a332-7497c7f143bb","content":"<p>instrument,</p>"}],"keys":["78054cba-fdb8-4f26-9a21-cdb552ae14fa"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a subject and a verb. When, as in this case, a subject (\"the strings inside the instrument\") is immediately followed by a main verb (\"are plucked\"), no punctuation is needed.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$a2816c7f$SATQ$, $SATQ$ed257044-3bd8-403a-8ea6-516cc4ebcac5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>American abstract artist Richard <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> his installations to make passersby keenly aware of how one&rsquo;s movements are affected by the physical features of one&rsquo;s environment, assembles large-scale steel plates into sculptures that dominate the outdoor spaces they occupy.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cb87de78-43d8-4d49-9707-2058c877d9cc","content":"<p>Serra is intending</p>"},{"id":"4549db2b-3b49-4730-8400-1aaab3cb5fcb","content":"<p>Serra, intends</p>"},{"id":"6ec0743e-08cc-481a-aabc-76416ef159a4","content":"<p>Serra, intending</p>"},{"id":"8831b1d2-58fe-4cd9-a60f-a29f075535dd","content":"<p>Serra intends</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6ec0743e-08cc-481a-aabc-76416ef159a4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. This choice pairs the comma after "Serra" with the comma after "environment" and uses the nonfinite present participle "intending" to correctly form a supplementary phrase describing the reaction Serra intends his sculptures to provoke. This supplementary phrase appears between the noun phrase that it modifies ("American abstract artist Richard Serra") and the finite present tense verb ("assembles"), which functions as the sentence&rsquo;s main verb and describes what Serra does.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present continuous tense verb "is intending" can&rsquo;t be used in this way in conjunction with the finite present tense verb "assembles," which already functions as the main verb in the sentence. Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present tense verb "intends" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to supplement the noun phrase "American abstract artist Richard Serra." Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present tense verb "intends" can&rsquo;t be used in this way in conjunction with the finite present tense verb "assembles," which already functions as the main verb in the sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a2816c7f","external_id":"ed257044-3bd8-403a-8ea6-516cc4ebcac5","vaultid":null,"uId":"06ae189e-7f7b-4b8a-8219-b842d7237f36","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>American abstract artist Richard <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> his installations to make passersby keenly aware of how one&rsquo;s movements are affected by the physical features of one&rsquo;s environment, assembles large-scale steel plates into sculptures that dominate the outdoor spaces they occupy.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cb87de78-43d8-4d49-9707-2058c877d9cc","content":"<p>Serra is intending</p>"},{"id":"4549db2b-3b49-4730-8400-1aaab3cb5fcb","content":"<p>Serra, intends</p>"},{"id":"6ec0743e-08cc-481a-aabc-76416ef159a4","content":"<p>Serra, intending</p>"},{"id":"8831b1d2-58fe-4cd9-a60f-a29f075535dd","content":"<p>Serra intends</p>"}],"keys":["6ec0743e-08cc-481a-aabc-76416ef159a4"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. This choice pairs the comma after \"Serra\" with the comma after \"environment\" and uses the nonfinite present participle \"intending\" to correctly form a supplementary phrase describing the reaction Serra intends his sculptures to provoke. This supplementary phrase appears between the noun phrase that it modifies (\"American abstract artist Richard Serra\") and the finite present tense verb (\"assembles\"), which functions as the sentence&rsquo;s main verb and describes what Serra does.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present continuous tense verb \"is intending\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way in conjunction with the finite present tense verb \"assembles,\" which already functions as the main verb in the sentence. Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present tense verb \"intends\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to supplement the noun phrase \"American abstract artist Richard Serra.\" Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present tense verb \"intends\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way in conjunction with the finite present tense verb \"assembles,\" which already functions as the main verb in the sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$a30567fd$SATQ$, $SATQ$ebfa7bf0-5d0f-4c25-a6e2-dc282a6fa3f2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$c4cee1fb-ee9f-4159-a450-1ecfbde49efa$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Nuhād al-Ḥaddād, known as Fairuz, was one of the most beloved Lebanese singers of the twentieth century. Her broad singing repertoire&mdash;which included traditional forms, such as the Arabic <em>qasida</em> and <em>maqam</em>, alongside modern pop and jazz styles&mdash;lent Fairuz a timeless, cross-generational appeal, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>&nbsp;her the moniker &ldquo;the soul of Lebanon.&rdquo;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cd49b367-b067-40a2-8063-e8614fa167ea","content":"<p>earned</p>"},{"id":"b1615efe-b7c2-4bb4-8896-0ea8fccb604a","content":"<p>had earned</p>"},{"id":"dea00c82-9a09-45db-bdfc-2ba1dabdf8bf","content":"<p>earning</p>"},{"id":"05d78aaa-5527-4551-a1ae-2c2dc7d3e499","content":"<p>earn</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dea00c82-9a09-45db-bdfc-2ba1dabdf8bf"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The word "earning" is being used to introduce an additional modifying phrase, which describes how Fairuz&rsquo;s repertoire lent her cross-generational appeal. When a verb ends in -ing and doesn&rsquo;t have a helper verb like "is" before it, it can be used to modify a noun or verb. This is appropriate here.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The underlined verb is being used to introduce additional descriptive information, which is set off from the rest of the sentence by a comma. Because there is no subject here, a finite form of the verb (like "earned") creates an error. We need another form of the verb that doesn&rsquo;t require a subject and can introduce descriptive information. Choice B is incorrect. The underlined verb is being used to introduce additional descriptive information, which is set off from the rest of the sentence by a comma. Because there is no subject here, a finite form of the verb (like "had earned") creates an error. We need another form of the verb that doesn&rsquo;t require a subject and can introduce descriptive information. Choice D is incorrect. The underlined verb is being used to introduce additional descriptive information, which is set off from the rest of the sentence by a comma. Because there is no subject here, a finite form of the verb (like "earn") creates an error. We need another form of the verb that doesn&rsquo;t require a subject and can introduce descriptive information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-M005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$2d9be744-cf36-4c08-896d-8ebc181b56ff$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a30567fd","external_id":"ebfa7bf0-5d0f-4c25-a6e2-dc282a6fa3f2","vaultid":"c4cee1fb-ee9f-4159-a450-1ecfbde49efa","uId":"c45ef3a5-dcfa-4a57-b619-78043db09f1e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Nuhād al-Ḥaddād, known as Fairuz, was one of the most beloved Lebanese singers of the twentieth century. Her broad singing repertoire&mdash;which included traditional forms, such as the Arabic <em>qasida</em> and <em>maqam</em>, alongside modern pop and jazz styles&mdash;lent Fairuz a timeless, cross-generational appeal, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>&nbsp;her the moniker &ldquo;the soul of Lebanon.&rdquo;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cd49b367-b067-40a2-8063-e8614fa167ea","content":"<p>earned</p>"},{"id":"b1615efe-b7c2-4bb4-8896-0ea8fccb604a","content":"<p>had earned</p>"},{"id":"dea00c82-9a09-45db-bdfc-2ba1dabdf8bf","content":"<p>earning</p>"},{"id":"05d78aaa-5527-4551-a1ae-2c2dc7d3e499","content":"<p>earn</p>"}],"keys":["dea00c82-9a09-45db-bdfc-2ba1dabdf8bf"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The word \"earning\" is being used to introduce an additional modifying phrase, which describes how Fairuz&rsquo;s repertoire lent her cross-generational appeal. When a verb ends in -ing and doesn&rsquo;t have a helper verb like \"is\" before it, it can be used to modify a noun or verb. This is appropriate here.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The underlined verb is being used to introduce additional descriptive information, which is set off from the rest of the sentence by a comma. Because there is no subject here, a finite form of the verb (like \"earned\") creates an error. We need another form of the verb that doesn&rsquo;t require a subject and can introduce descriptive information. Choice B is incorrect. The underlined verb is being used to introduce additional descriptive information, which is set off from the rest of the sentence by a comma. Because there is no subject here, a finite form of the verb (like \"had earned\") creates an error. We need another form of the verb that doesn&rsquo;t require a subject and can introduce descriptive information. Choice D is incorrect. The underlined verb is being used to introduce additional descriptive information, which is set off from the rest of the sentence by a comma. Because there is no subject here, a finite form of the verb (like \"earn\") creates an error. We need another form of the verb that doesn&rsquo;t require a subject and can introduce descriptive information.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-M005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"2d9be744-cf36-4c08-896d-8ebc1